A||||4||kel||mama ain't happy
Z||000000||kel||09-03-99||03:28 AM||||I went to look at my car yesterday and the nose looks pretty good on top, but there is  a small ripple in the black plastic air dam opening.  The part I am really not happy about is the hood.  There is not one mark on the hood, but it must have gotten slightly bowed in the accident(it's not as even with the fender on the driver's side) and it was pushed back about 1/4 inch.  (I discovered after I took it to the shop, that the headlights would not open the whole way.)  Even the appraiser from our insurance company missed it.  Well the shop adjusted the headlights so that they open now, but the hood is still pushed back, thus leaving me with a wider gap than I had before between the front edge of the hood and the fascia, and also that very slight bowing of the hood on the driver's side.  He says there is nothing he can do about that.  I questioned him about all of it, and he says he doesn't think the hood was effected.  Duh... what'd I just say?  I also laid down on the ground to look under the nose and he seemed a little surprised.  He kept asking if I wanted my husband to look at it.  I think he thinks I'm being too picky and I don't think he appreciates a woman's opinion on car matters.  I am losing faith quickly and I am tired of being without my car...it's been 3 weeks now. Our insurance is paying the bulk of this and this shop has the money from them and has already done all the work underneath the bumper, so I don't even know if I can change shops now that I'm in mid-stream.  My husband knows how unhappy I am with the whole situation right now and is going to go look at it this weekend.  Maybe his opinion will be taken seriously.<br>Did I say before that I was putting my trust in this guy?  Could be I'm a little too trusting...     
Z||000001||batboy||09-03-99||05:04 AM||||You might try complaining to your insurance company and see if they can put some pressure on the body shop. As much as they charge, they ought to be able to repair your car back to your satifaction. The trouble is, if he's already been paid, he'll look for ways to get rid of you without doing anymore work. Trust me, there are plenty of Fiero noses and hoods out there. Sounds like you need to quit trying to repair the old ones and get undamaged parts to replace them. The Fiero Store sells front fascias and usually Kick Hill or Ed Parks has some good used body parts.
Z||000002||88 Fiero||09-03-99||08:27 AM||||bat is right!!!! you have to go to your insurance company and complain about it...also don't forget to check to see if the frame is bent..sometimes this is the part that is also overlook...have the bodyshop take off all the panels and see if the frame is bent...if so have them straighten out the frame...reason why I am asking...when I got my '85 I notice that the right side door doesn't close properly and the right fender and the door gap is very close and sometimes hitting...when I took those apart I notice that the frame was a little bent and I was pissed!!! because the guy who sold me the car said that he hardly drive the car and has not been in the accident...So check that part tho'.....good luck!!!!
Z||000003||theogre||09-03-99||01:20 PM||||also file complaints with your states insurance regulators. This guy could be running a scam. (High estimates and billing but no real work getting done, and likely skipping some parts that don't show easily.)<p>Likely the hood hinge is bent thus holding it back. All they should have to do is straighten or adjust around it. If the hinge IS NOT bent, then you have frame damage that has not been addressed.<p>Find another cat so you can see what the hinge should look like. You should see that the lower section is straight and bolts pretty flat to the car. If yours looks diferant it's bent. Bent hood hinges are common on Fieros that get whacked. Whithout seeing the car, I can't tell you much more than that.
Z||000004||RFMike||09-03-99||05:02 PM||||Kel,<br>My GT was the victim of a parking lot hit and run to the front end.  The hood was pushed back.  The hood hinges were bent, just as the  Ogre said, and it did cause the hood to be out of alignment with the driver's fender (coincidence for the same side).  The hood looked bent, but wasn't.  When my pal with the shop straightened the hinges, it was almost as good as new.  He took a look at the frame, and did some minor adjustment to the frame.  It's perfect now.  The hood is probably not bent.  Fiberglass tends to want to return to it's original shape.  Also, the fender can also be adjusted up slightly to match the hood. <br>Be patient.  If it was one of the nicer ones around, it will be worth the wait.  If its not right when you get it back, it will bug you forever.
